00:00:00.069 --> 00:00:02.879
Mind your decisions I'm Presh Talwalkar

00:00:03.819 --> 00:00:06.179
In a square with a side length of one

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:11.550
Construct two quarter circles and then inscribe a circle between the quarter circles

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:17.219
The challenge is to solve for the circles radius. I think Pradeep Hebbar for the suggestion

00:00:17.949 --> 00:00:24.239
Pause the video. If you'd like to give this problem a try and when you're ready keep watching to learn how to solve this problem

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:42.489
We'll get started by drawing lines between the corner of the square and the tangent point on the inscribed circle

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:49.180
The first thing we'll do is we'll prove that this intersection point all is the center of the circle

00:00:50.239 --> 00:00:52.808
We'll get started by considering one quarter circle

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:55.900
consider the tangent point and the tangent line

00:00:56.629 --> 00:01:01.749
The perpendicular to this tangent line will pass through the center of the quarter circle

00:01:02.059 --> 00:01:06.189
Point A and it will pass through the center of the inscribed circle

00:01:07.399 --> 00:01:10.239
However, we don't know the exact location of O

00:01:11.630 --> 00:01:15.999
Now, let's consider the other quarter circle. We'll do a similar construction

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:20.320
We have the tangent line and then the perpendicular to the tangent line

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:23.499
This will pass through the center of the quarter circle

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:29.919
which is the corner of the square and then it will also pass through the centre of the inscribed circle O .

00:01:30.649 --> 00:01:32.649
If we put these observations together

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:35.560
the intersection point of these two lines

00:01:36.049 --> 00:01:41.229
Must contain the center O and therefore this is the center of the inscribed circle

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:44.500
Now let's focus on this quarter circle

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:49.929
The square has a side length of one which means the radius of this quarter circle is equal to one

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:58.869
Suppose the inscribed circle has a radius equal to R. This means the distance between the two centers is 1 minus R

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:01.600
We then drop a perpendicular

00:02:01.909 --> 00:02:08.439
which will be a radius of the inscribed circle and this will bisect the side of the square into two lengths of 0.5

00:02:08.959 --> 00:02:13.389
We thus have the following right triangle and we can use the Gougu theorem

00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:17.889
0.5 squared plus R squared is equal to the quantity 1 minus R squared

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:25.269
Well, then do some simplifying algebra the R squares cancel out and then we can routinely solve for R

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:29.320
We carefully calculate that R is equal to

00:02:29.989 --> 00:02:33.189
0.375 and that's equal to 3 over 8

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:36.279
Think this is a fun little problem

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:45.090
Thanks for making mind your decisions one of the best channels on YouTube as always. Thanks for watching and thanks for your support

